{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB11978 - MONDO_18434",
  "verbalization": "Glasdegib is implicated in the treatment of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) with specific genetic mutations, such as Npm1 somatic mutations and T(9;11)(p22;q23). This relationship is supported by multiple pathways where Glasdegib is linked to AML subtypes through its indication. The evidence suggests that Glasdegib may act as a therapeutic agent for AML with Npm1 mutations, and in some cases, it may also be effective for AML with T(9;11)(p22;q23) translocations, as indicated by its use in combination with other drugs like Quizartinib and Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin. The overlapping use of Glasdegib in different AML subtypes implies a potential mechanism of action that is relevant across these disease states. The integration of these pathways highlights the possibility that Glasdegib may target shared molecular pathways or cellular processes that are dysregulated in AML, thereby supporting its role in the treatment of this condition.",
